.TH ADDCCTL 1 "" "addcctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
addcctl \- operations of a Samba Active Directory domain controller, as JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B addcctl
.I object action
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RI < input.json
.br
.B addcctl schema
.br
.B addcctl mcp
.RB [ \-\-read\-only ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B addcctl
runs on a Samba AD domain controller, as root, and performs one operation:
it reads a JSON object on standard input and writes a JSON object on
standard output. It reads the configuration through Samba's loadparm and the
directory from the local sam.ldb, so it needs no domain credentials.
.PP
It is the core of cockpit-samba-dc: the Cockpit page, the Ansible collection
lineadicomando.samba_dc and the MCP server call it.
.PP
.B addcctl schema
prints every operation with the JSON Schema of its input.
.PP
.B addcctl mcp
is an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server on standard input and output, one
JSON-RPC message per line: one tool per operation, named
.IR object _ action
(dashes become underscores), with the operation's input. A tool that changes
something also takes "check": true, the preview of
.BR \-\-check .
Every change is written to syslog (facility authpriv, tag addcctl-mcp) with
the user who started the server and the passwords masked. It is meant to be
started by an MCP client through SSH and sudo.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report what the operation would change, in the "commands" field of the
output, and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-read\-only
With
.BR mcp ,
offer only the operations that change nothing.
.SH OPERATIONS
Names are sAMAccountNames. The input of each operation, with its fields and
their meaning, is in
.BR "addcctl schema" .
.TP
.B user list\fR, \fBuser get
The domain users, with their groups, status, lockout and expiry.
.TP
.B user create\fR, \fBuser set\fR, \fBuser password\fR, \fBuser unlock\fR, \fBuser delete
Create a user; bring it to the given settings (a new name also renames it in
the shared folders); set its password; unlock it; delete it. Passwords come
in the input and are never written out. The built-in accounts are protected.
.TP
.B group list\fR, \fBgroup get\fR, \fBgroup create\fR, \fBgroup set\fR, \fBgroup delete
The groups with their members, and their changes.
.TP
.B computer list\fR, \fBcomputer get\fR, \fBcomputer create\fR, \fBcomputer delete
The computer accounts; create one before the computer joins; delete one with
its DNS records (not a domain controller).
.TP
.B ou list\fR, \fBou get\fR, \fBou create\fR, \fBou set\fR, \fBou delete
Organizational units and the default containers; create, rename, move or
describe an OU, delete it (one that is not empty with "recursive"). The
default containers are never renamed, moved or deleted.
.TP
.B dns zones\fR, \fBdns list\fR, \fBdns set\fR, \fBdns delete
The DNS zones of the domain and their records, written locally in sam.ldb:
"dns set" makes the records of one type at one name exactly the values
given (A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, PTR, SRV, TXT), "dns delete" removes some or all
of them. The records the domain depends on are never changed.
.TP
.B gpo list\fR, \fBgpo get\fR, \fBgpo link\fR, \fBgpo unlink
The Group Policy objects and where they are linked; link one to the domain
or an OU, enforce or disable the link, remove it, written to the
container's gPLink in sam.ldb. The default GPOs and the drive-mapping GPO
stay linked where they are.
.TP
.B home provision\fR, \fBhome delete
A user's home directory under /srv/samba/home, mapped at logon; clear it, and
with "delete_data" remove its folder.
.TP
.B domain password\-policy\fR, \fBdomain set\-password\-policy
The domain password and lockout settings; change them, with the ranges and
checks of "samba\-tool domain passwordsettings set" (the lockout window at
most the lockout duration).
.TP
.B pso list\fR, \fBpso get\fR, \fBpso create\fR, \fBpso set\fR, \fBpso delete
Fine-grained password policies (Password Settings Objects): the settings of
the domain policy, a precedence and the users and groups they apply to. A
new PSO takes the domain's settings for those left out; "user get" returns
the policy that applies to a user.
.TP
.B domain info
The domain and its controllers: names, Samba version, functional levels, FSMO
role owners, the processes of this DC. Read locally, with no network query.
.TP
.B domain check
Run health checks and report their exit code and output: "samba\-tool dbcheck
\-\-cross\-ncs" (nothing is fixed), "samba\-tool ntacl sysvolcheck", "testparm
\-s"; "checks" picks some of them.
.TP
.B domain sysvol\-reset
Reset the ACLs of sysvol ("samba\-tool ntacl sysvolreset").
.TP
.B domain setup\-check
Whether this host is a domain controller already and, when it is not, the
prerequisites of a new domain (smb.conf, a static address, the host name,
free ports, time, disk space), each with its state (ok, warning, error) and
its fix, and the values proposed for it.
.TP
.B domain provision
Provision a new domain with this host as its only domain controller: realm,
netbios_domain, admin_password, dns_forwarder. Refused when a domain exists or
a prerequisite fails. Stops and masks smbd, nmbd and winbind, keeps Debian's
smb.conf as smb.conf.dist, runs Samba's provisioning in the process (the
password never in an argv; its messages in
/var/log/samba/addcctl\-provision.log), sets up /etc/hosts, /etc/krb5.conf,
nsswitch.conf and chrony, starts samba\-ad\-dc and points /etc/resolv.conf at
it. Not served by "addcctl mcp".
.TP
.B backup list\fR, \fBbackup create
The offline backups of the domain in /var/backups/samba\-dc; make one
("samba\-tool domain backup offline"). The directory and the archives are
readable by root only: they hold every password hash. Restoring is done by
hand, with "samba\-tool domain backup restore".
.TP
.B share list\fR, \fBshare get
The shared folders under /srv/samba/shares, with their access list and drive
mapping.
.TP
.B share set
Create a shared folder, or bring it to the given settings: name, access,
access_mode, comment, browseable, drive. Settings left out keep their value.
.TP
.B share delete
Remove a shared folder and its drive mapping; with "delete_data", its folder
too.
.TP
.B share rename\-principal
Follow the rename of a user or group in the shared folders that list it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when the operation fails; 2 for an unknown operation or an
input that is not valid. On failure the output is {"error": message} and the
message is also written to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
echo '{"name": "Projects", "access": [{"name": "Staff", "level": "write"}],
       "drive": {"letter": "P"}}' | addcctl share set \-\-check
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR samba\-tool (8),
.BR net (8)
